.TH TGAMMA 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
tgamma, tgammaf, tgammal, lgamma, lgammaf, lgammal, lgamma_r, signgam \- Gamma functions
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double tgamma(double " x );
.br
.BI "float tgammaf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double tgammal(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double lgamma(double " x );
.br
.BI "float lgammaf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double lgammal(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double lgamma_r(double " x ", int *" signp );
.PP
.B "extern int signgam;"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR tgamma ()
function returns the value of the true Gamma function
.I \[*G](x)
of
.IR x ,
defined for
.I x > 0
by the Euler integral
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
          /\[if]    x\-1   \-t
\[*G](x) = |    t   * e   dt
        /0
.EE
.RE
.PP
and extended to the rest of the real line by the recurrence
.BI \[*G]( "x+1" ") = " x " * \[*G](" x )
and the reflection formula
.BR "\[*G](x) * \[*G](1\-x) = \[*pi] / sin(\[*pi]*x)" .
For every positive integer
.IR n ,
.PP
.RS 4
.BI tgamma( n ") == (" "n\-1" ")!"
.RE
.PP
so
.BR "tgamma(1) = tgamma(2) = 1" ,
.BR "tgamma(3) = 2" ,
.BR "tgamma(4) = 6" ,
.BR "tgamma(5) = 24" ,
and so on.  At the half-integer
.B 0.5
the value
.BI tgamma( 0.5 ") = sqrt(\[*pi])"
\(em the famous Euler identity \(em is recovered from the reflection
formula.
.PP
The
.BR lgamma ()
function returns the natural logarithm of the absolute value of the
Gamma function,
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
lgamma(x) = ln |\[*G](x)|
.EE
.RE
.PP
and additionally stores the sign of
.I \[*G](x)
(either
.B +1
or
.BR \-1 )
in the global
.B int
variable
.B signgam
declared in
.BR <math.h> .
Because Gamma grows so rapidly that
.B tgamma(171.0)
already overflows
.BR double ,
.BR lgamma ()
is the practical interface for evaluating Gamma at large positive
arguments: the result of
.B \[*G](x)
can be reconstructed (when finite) as
.BI signgam " * exp(lgamma(" x "))" .
.PP
The
.BR lgamma_r ()
function is a thread-safe (re-entrant) variant of
.BR lgamma ()
that stores the sign in
.BI * signp
instead of writing to the shared global
.BR signgam .
It is a BSD extension; multithreaded callers should always prefer it
to
.BR lgamma ().
.PP
The
.BR tgammaf (),
.BR tgammal (),
.BR lgammaf (),
and
.BR lgammal ()
variants take and return
.B float
or
.B "long double"
values; they otherwise have the same semantics as the
.B double
versions.
.PP
.SS Substrate Implementation
The Substrate libm computes
.BR tgamma ()
using a Lanczos approximation with
.B g = 7
and
.B "n = 9"
coefficients (the standard Wikipedia tabulation), giving roughly 15
significant decimal digits for arguments in the moderate range.  For
.I "x < 0.5"
the reflection formula
.BI "\[*G](x) = \[*pi] / (sin(\[*pi]*x) * \[*G](1\-x))"
is applied recursively.  Arguments greater than
.B 171.624
are short-circuited to
.B +HUGE_VAL
with
.B FE_OVERFLOW
because the true result exceeds
.BR DBL_MAX .
.PP
.BR lgamma_r ()
\(em and therefore
.BR lgamma ()
\(em defers to
.BR tgamma ()
followed by
.BR log ()
for
.IR "|x| < 170" ,
which inherits the Lanczos accuracy.  For
.I "x \[>=] 170"
where
.BR tgamma ()
would overflow, the implementation switches to a single-term
Stirling expansion:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ln \[*G](x) ~ (x \- 0.5) ln x  \-  x  +  0.5 ln(2\[*pi])  +  1 / (12x)
.EE
.RE
.PP
This is sufficient for the large-argument regime where the
.BR 1/(12x)
correction is the only term that matters; callers needing higher
accuracy at moderate
.I x
should rely on the Lanczos branch and not attempt to evaluate
Stirling directly.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR tgamma ()
returns the value of
.I \[*G](x)
and
.BR lgamma ()
(or
.BR lgamma_r ())
returns
.IR "ln |\[*G](x)|" .
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN,
.BR tgamma (),
.BR lgamma (),
and
.BR lgamma_r ()
all return a NaN.
.BR lgamma_r ()
additionally stores
.B +1
in
.BI * signp .
.PP
If
.I x
is
.B +0
or
.BR \-0 ,
.BR tgamma ()
returns
.B +\[if]
or
.BR \-\[if] ,
respectively, and raises
.BR FE_DIVBYZERO ;
.BR lgamma ()
returns
.B +\[if]
and raises
.BR FE_DIVBYZERO .
.PP
If
.I x
is a negative integer (a pole of Gamma),
.BR tgamma ()
returns NaN and raises
.BR FE_INVALID ;
.BR lgamma ()
returns
.B +\[if]
and raises
.BR FE_DIVBYZERO .
.PP
If
.I x
is positive infinity,
.BR tgamma ()
returns
.B +\[if]
and
.BR lgamma ()
returns
.BR +\[if] .
If
.I x
is negative infinity,
.BR tgamma ()
returns NaN and raises
.B FE_INVALID
while
.BR lgamma ()
returns
.BR +\[if] .
.PP
If the magnitude of the result of
.BR tgamma ()
would exceed
.BR DBL_MAX
(approximately, when
.IR "x > 171.624" ),
.B +HUGE_VAL
is returned and
.B FE_OVERFLOW
is raised.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Domain error: \fIx\fP is a negative integer or \-\[if]
.BR tgamma ()
returns NaN and raises
.BR FE_INVALID .
.TP
Pole error: \fIx\fP is +0, \-0, or a negative integer
.BR tgamma ()
returns
.B \(+-\[if]
and raises
.BR FE_DIVBYZERO ;
.BR lgamma ()
returns
.B +\[if]
and raises
.BR FE_DIVBYZERO .
.TP
Overflow error: \fIx\fP is too large
.BR tgamma ()
returns
.B +HUGE_VAL
and raises
.BR FE_OVERFLOW .
The Substrate implementation triggers this guard for
.IR "x > 171.624" .
.PP
None of these functions sets
.IR errno ;
errors are reported via the floating-point environment only.  Use
.BR feclearexcept (3)
and
.BR fetestexcept (3)
to detect them.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR tgamma (),
.BR tgammaf (),
.BR tgammal (),
.BR lgamma_r ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
T{
.BR lgamma (),
.BR lgammaf (),
.BR lgammal ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Unsafe race:signgam
.TE
.PP
.BR lgamma ()
and its
.BR f / l
variants write to the shared global
.B signgam
on every call.  Multithreaded code that needs the sign of
.I \[*G](x)
must use
.BR lgamma_r ().
.SH STANDARDS
.BR tgamma (),
.BR lgamma (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of special
arguments follows C99 Annex F.10.4.  The global
.B signgam
is specified by XSI / POSIX.1-2008.
.BR lgamma_r ()
is a BSD extension provided by Substrate, glibc, and most other
modern C libraries; it is not part of POSIX.  The accuracy of all of
these functions is implementation-defined; see
.B "Substrate Implementation"
above.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program verifies
.BI tgamma( 0.5 ") = sqrt(\[*pi])"
and
.BI tgamma( 5 ") = 4! = 24" ,
prints the natural log of
.BR 10! ,
and demonstrates how
.B signgam
is updated by
.BR lgamma ():
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double half = tgamma(0.5);
    double fact4 = tgamma(5.0);          /* 4! = 24            */
    double l10  = lgamma(11.0);          /* ln(10!) ~ 15.1044  */

    printf("tgamma(0.5) = %.10f\en", half);
    printf("sqrt(pi)    = %.10f\en", sqrt(M_PI));
    printf("|tgamma(0.5) \- sqrt(pi)| = %g\en",
           fabs(half \- sqrt(M_PI)));

    printf("tgamma(5)   = %.1f  (expect 24)\en", fact4);
    printf("lgamma(11)  = %.6f  signgam=%+d\en", l10, signgam);

    /* Negative non-integer: Gamma is negative on (\-1, 0). */
    double n = lgamma(\-0.5);
    printf("lgamma(\-0.5) = %.6f  signgam=%+d\en", n, signgam);

    /* Reentrant variant: no global side effect. */
    int sgn;
    double r = lgamma_r(\-0.5, &sgn);
    printf("lgamma_r(\-0.5) = %.6f  sign=%+d\en", r, sgn);

    /* Special values. */
    printf("tgamma(1)        = %g\en",    tgamma(1.0));
    printf("tgamma(0)        = %g\en",    tgamma(0.0));
    printf("isnan(tgamma(\-1)) = %d\en",   isnan(tgamma(\-1.0)));
    printf("tgamma(+INF)     = %g\en",    tgamma(INFINITY));
    printf("isnan(tgamma(\-INF)) = %d\en", isnan(tgamma(\-INFINITY)));

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A representative output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
tgamma(0.5) = 1.7724538509
sqrt(pi)    = 1.7724538509
|tgamma(0.5) \- sqrt(pi)| = 0
tgamma(5)   = 24.0  (expect 24)
lgamma(11)  = 15.104413  signgam=+1
lgamma(\-0.5) = 1.265512  signgam=\-1
lgamma_r(\-0.5) = 1.265512  sign=\-1
tgamma(1)        = 1
tgamma(0)        = inf
isnan(tgamma(\-1)) = 1
tgamma(+INF)     = inf
isnan(tgamma(\-INF)) = 1
.EE
.RE
